SOAPBOX: Poor conditions coupled with an unusually cold, snowy and icy
SOAPBOX: storm during the entire contest period made for many challenges.
SOAPBOX: A few hours in, the resonant frequency of my inverted L started
SOAPBOX: drifting lower from the ever-heavier ice coating. This was
SOAPBOX: disappointing because I had gotten it matched well with a shunt
SOAPBOX: coil at the feedpoint, obviating the need to use a tuner in the
SOAPBOX: shack. By Saturday afternoon, I had to put the tuner back in
SOAPBOX: line. Two new line noises appeared by the start of the contest,
SOAPBOX: making three in all. One was intermittent, but the other two
SOAPBOX: were constant. I do not know if that was because of the
SOAPBOX: increased electrical load due to the extreme, for Texas, cold or
SOAPBOX: ice on the power lines or something else. But with three noise
SOAPBOX: sources, I could only cancel one at a time. At most times, every
SOAPBOX: Beverage had substantial noise. I had never before seen that.
SOAPBOX: As a result of these difficulties, my operating time was the
SOAPBOX: lowest in years. I had high levels of fatigue from straining to
SOAPBOX: copy more than the usual number of weaker signals. N1MM+ says I
SOAPBOX: operated 15 hours, 43 minutes, but I had many breaks under 30
SOAPBOX: minutes long. No doubt I put in fewer than 15 hours. Two big
SOAPBOX: surprises the first night were (1) working a few stations in the
SOAPBOX: former Yugoslavia and (2) a very strong PP5JR calling me. I
SOAPBOX: never heard any other Europeans from outside that area. I heard
SOAPBOX: no Europeans at all the second night. Also, never heard any JAs,
SOAPBOX: although I might have heard a trace of one calling a West
SOAPBOX: Coaster. I finally heard a lone Washington station the second
SOAPBOX: night. He called and worked VA7MM, who couild never hear me.
SOAPBOX: VE7ZO had called me earlier, so it was WA I needed. Two
SOAPBOX: surprises the second night were two callers: (1) very loud
SOAPBOX: YV5JDP and (2) moderately loud PY5XH.
